## Test Anti-Pattern Audit: `SerializerTests.cs`

**Summary: 4 Critical · 1 High · 2 Medium · 0 Low**

This suite is dominated by **self-referential / tautological assertions** — tests that assert `output == input` on operations fed no-op inputs, or on round-trips that silently discard data. Four of eight tests can never fail regardless of whether the production code is correct.

---

### 🔴 Critical — False Confidence

#### 1. `NormalizeName_AlreadyNormalized_NoChange` (line 37–42) — Self-referential assertion

The input `"alice"` is *already* lowercase and trimmed. `Normalize` does `Trim().ToLowerInvariant()`, so the output is trivially identical to the input. The test can never detect a broken normalization.

```csharp
// BEFORE — tautological: no-op input guarantees input == output
var name = "alice";
var result = NameNormalizer.Normalize(name);
Assert.AreEqual(name, result);

// AFTER — verify actual transformation
var result = NameNormalizer.Normalize("  ALICE  ");
Assert.AreEqual("alice", result);
```

#### 2. `UpperCase_ToUpper_Works` (line 72–77) — Self-referential assertion

`"HELLO".ToUpper()` is a no-op. The test will pass even if `ToUpper()` returned the string unchanged (which is exactly what happens).

```csharp
// BEFORE — input already uppercase, no transformation tested
var input = "HELLO";
var result = input.ToUpper();
Assert.AreEqual(input, result);

// AFTER — verify actual case transformation
var result = "hello".ToUpper();
Assert.AreEqual("HELLO", result);
```

#### 3. `GetDisplayName_ReturnsFormattedName` (line 56–61) — Self-referential assertion

Asserts `user.Name == user.GetDisplayName()`. Since `GetDisplayName()` returns `Name`, this is `x == x`. The name says "FormattedName" but nothing is formatted and the test can't detect whether it ever would be.

```csharp
// BEFORE — asserts a field against itself via a pass-through method
var user = new UserDto { Name = "Alice", Age = 30 };
var display = user.GetDisplayName();
Assert.AreEqual(user.Name, display);

// AFTER — assert against a known expected value (exposes the contract)
var user = new UserDto { Name = "Alice", Age = 30 };
var display = user.GetDisplayName();
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", display);
// If GetDisplayName is *intended* to format (e.g., "Alice (30)"),
// fix both the method and the expected value.
```

#### 4. `MapToDto_MapBack_RoundTrip` (line 45–53) — Self-referential round-trip hiding data loss

The mapper is **lossy**: `ToDto` drops `Id` and `Email`, hardcodes `Age = 0`. `ToEntity` drops `Id` and `Email`. The round-trip test pretends the mapping is lossless. Worse, `entity.Id` is `1` but `backToEntity.Id` is `0`, and `entity.Email` is `"a@b.com"` but `backToEntity.Email` is `""` — so the assertions on lines 50 and 52 **should actually fail**, meaning this test either doesn't run or was never checked.

```csharp
// BEFORE — round-trip hides that the mapping is lossy
var dto = Mapper.ToDto(entity);
var backToEntity = Mapper.ToEntity(dto);
Assert.AreEqual(entity.Id, backToEntity.Id);       // 1 != 0
Assert.AreEqual(entity.FullName, backToEntity.FullName);
Assert.AreEqual(entity.Email, backToEntity.Email);  // "a@b.com" != ""

// AFTER — test each direction independently against expected values
var entity = new UserEntity { Id = 1, FullName = "Alice Smith", Email = "a@b.com" };
var dto = Mapper.ToDto(entity);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice Smith", dto.Name);
Assert.AreEqual(0, dto.Age);   // document the known limitation

var dto2 = new UserDto { Name = "Bob", Age = 25 };
var result = Mapper.ToEntity(dto2);
Assert.AreEqual("Bob", result.FullName);
Assert.AreEqual(0, result.Id);     // document the known default
Assert.AreEqual("", result.Email); // document the known default
```

---

### 🟠 High

#### 5. `Validate_ValidInput_ReturnsSameInput` (line 63–69) — Self-referential assertion (weaker variant)

`ValidateEmail` returns its input on success, so `Assert.AreEqual(input, result)` is `x == x` by contract. More importantly, there is **no test for the rejection path** — the `ArgumentException` thrown on invalid input is completely unverified.

```csharp
// AFTER — test the actual validation behavior
[TestMethod]
public void ValidateEmail_Valid_ReturnsInput()
{
    var result = Validator.ValidateEmail("hello@world.com");
    Assert.AreEqual("hello@world.com", result);
}

[TestMethod]
public void ValidateEmail_MissingAt_Throws()
{
    Assert.ThrowsException<ArgumentException>(
        () => Validator.ValidateEmail("not-an-email"));
}
```

---

### 🟡 Medium

| # | Test | Issue | Fix |
